    "created_at": "2026-07-10T14:59:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-07-10T15:00:59Z",
    "body": "With current unified stack, moving it all to workgroup memory is a bad idea. Because it will occupy the whole LDS and limit occupancy.\r\n\r\nSee register allocation of baseline:\r\n<img width=\"358\" height=\"244\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bca1aa32-0ea9-457d-a990-2bd91896951b\" />\r\n\r\nAnd with whole stack in workgroup memory:\r\n<img width=\"359\" height=\"239\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5045f1f4-3fe5-462d-b522-6628a2beee73\" />\r\n\r\nNote how occupancy is lower in second case.\r\n\r\nThe original optimisation idea was to move just the *half* of the stack so as to allow 50% occupancy. (moving more to the LDS will lower occupancy further which is undesirable):\r\n<img width=\"359\" height=\"244\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/687ec44f-cd7c-413e-bac1-2cdf99943db2\" />\r\n\r\nHowever, it seems that moving a half of the unified stack is not cheap. I tested with an `if` and a `select` for access: both variants were slower than the baseline. So, it seems to me like a dead end. Perhaps LDS can be used in another way.\r\n   ",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
